working a retail job is generally less  than ideal most people working retail  jobs have no other choice and they're  simply trying to make ends meet  considering this it's not surprising  that most retail giants have terrible  employee retention with walmart ranking  the bottom 25 of employers  but there is one grocery chain called  heb that retail employees not only love  working at but they never want to leave  in fact heb ranks in the top five  percent in terms of employee retention  and they boast an a plus rating compared  to walmart c minus at heb it's not  uncommon to see employees start off as  card pushers and baggers in high school  who slowly work their way up to cashier  shift manager and store manager by the  time they're in their 40s  you couldn't even pay these guys to  leave as 50 of employees said that they  wouldn't leave heb even if they were  offered a higher paying job  but this isn't too surprising when you  consider that heb is not only one of the  best retail employers but the 10th best  employer in america period that's 21  spots higher than apple so here's how a  retail store became one of the best  places to work in america  taking a look back the story of heb  dates back to the early 1900s to a  couple named charles and florence butt  the family was originally from tennessee  but they decided to move to caribou  texas because charles was suffering from  tuberculosis and they believed that a  drier climate would help his condition  after moving to texas in an effort to  support the family financially florence  began a small grocery store on the  ground floor of their apartment in 1905.  renting the ground floor cost her nine  dollars per month and she used the rest  of her savings which was 60 dollars to  stock the store  initially she sold her grocery items  door to door which was particularly hard  given that many customers were rude and  unforgiving labeling her as a peddler  but as she slowly built up a small group  of loyal customers florence was able to  give up her door-to-door days and stick  to the store  luckily florence had three children and  two stepchildren who could help around  the store in the early days but florence  would end up running the store by  herself as world war 1 rolled around  around the same time her husband charles  senior would pass away in 1915 so  florence was truly on her own for many  years  fortunately two of her children charles  jr and howard would eventually return to  the business  charles jr returned to 1917 and howard  returned 1919 after serving in world war  one for several years charles jr and  howard would leave the grocery store  together but in 1926 charles jr would  also succumb to tuberculosis just like  his father which left the store to  howard and his mother howard attempted  to expand the store by opening three  more locations in the same area but none  of these panned out  it wasn't until howard took a loan for  38 thousand dollars and open stores  outside of kerbel that he found some  success  as for florence she stuck around until  1934 but as she saw that howard had a  good grip on the business she stepped  away from the business but she never  stepped away from the community florence  strongly believed that it was the  community that got her family through  their roughest time and she was  eternally grateful for that so she did  everything she could to give back to the  community she opened the baptist church  in curveball and paid for the pastor's  salary for several years she also never  thought twice to give to those in need  and that brings us into the first core  value that drives employees at heb which  is community service to this day heb is  dedicated to helping every community  they serve  in fact they have so many community  oriented programs that would take hours  to go through all of them so i'll just  come over the most significant ones  starting with supplier diversity  instead of sticking with large big box  suppliers heb goes out of their way to  support local businesses run by women  minorities veterans disabled individuals  and the lgbt community another heb  community program is operation  appreciation heb regularly sends out  care packages to active service members  and they even build houses for veterans  who are injured while on duty another  heb program is hunger relief which  donates absurd amounts of food to food  banks in 2018 alone heb donated 32  million pounds of food to food banks in  texas and mexico which works out to 25  million meals yet another http program  is disaster relief which focuses on  donating money emerging supplies  drinking water and food to victims of  natural disasters all of that is just  scratching the surface of hp's community  involvement  they also have programs related to  sustainability education first  responders diversity and much more and  on top of all of that heb has been  donating five percent of their pre-tax  income to charity for over 80 years  given heb substantial community  involvement heb employees often feel  like they're not just working a job but  that they're truly serving their  community and this has done wonders for  hb's employees satisfaction and spirit  anyway going back to howard he relocated  the headquarters of the business to  harlingen texas in the late 1920s and he  would open the company's first  distribution center in harlingen howard  began a tradition of supporting local  suppliers by opening the harlingen  cannery which featured texas grown  texas-packed food over the next several  years howard opened a handful of stores  across texas in laredo corpus christi  austin and san antonio  these stores were generally about 10 000  square feet on average so much bigger  than the grocery store his mom used to  run but just as everything was  progressing forward a massive hurricane  would destroy the company's distribution  center in 1933 despite suffering a  massive loss himself howard donated food  and water to the disaster victims and  the following year was the first time  that howard would donate five percent of  the company's income to charity with the  establishment of the h.e butt foundation  in 1935 howard changed the name of the  grocery store from cc but grocery to  aichi butt grocery he would eventually  shorten this to heb in 1946  as heb continued to grow howard got his  children howard jr and charles involved  in the business  charles describes that he was working at  the store by the time he was eight and  that he would consistently work friday  afternoons and saturdays throughout his  childhood howard spent a lot of time  trying to convince charles and howard jr  to stay at the business and though he  was successful with charles howard jr  would go on his own path  when they grew up howard jr became a lay  minister while charles went to wharton  business school before returning to the  business  and when howard retired in 1971 charles  took over as president and he's been  president's ceo ever since  to this day heb is a private family  business and they don't have a bunch of  wall street investors gobbling up their  stock  but this doesn't mean that heb is only  owned by the butt family while they  don't want to share heb with wall street  they're more than happy to share the  company with their employees and that  brings us to heb second core principle  which is treating employees like family  given the nature of retail it's quite  difficult to pay high salaries due to  low margins but heb does everything they  can to give their employees the best  benefits and perks they offer great 401k  plans retirement plans and entrance  options they also offer their employees  a 10 discount on all hpv brand products  but by far the coolest perk i think is  their stock compensation plan basically  all heb employees are eligible for stock  compensation regardless of what work  they do  so it's not some sort of elitist  executive thing to receive stock  compensation heb only has three  requirements  you have to be at least 21 years old  work at least one year at heb and you  must put in at least 1 000 hours per  year in other words you must be at least  a part-time employee as long as you take  all these boxes you're eligible to  receive a stock bonus worth 3 of your  annual income plus 100 worth of stock  every year  these types of benefits for retail  employees are basically unheard of  walmart offers a 15 discount on their  stock for their employees up to 1800  but i'm not familiar with any company  that gives their retail employees stock  compensation leave a comment down below  if you know another company that does  this  by giving juicy perks and benefits to  virtually all of their employees heb  makes it clear that they highly value it  and cherish their employees which has  only further cemented their employee  loyalty anyway going back to charles he  opened the company's first limited  assortment store called heb pantry foods  in 1988.  these stores centered on selling basic  groceries for low prices and were  generally much larger than traditional  igbs coming in at 24 000 to 30 000  square feet this quickly became the  bread and butter of the company and is  what most people associate with heb  today  in the early 1990s charles also  experimented with a new style of store  called the central market  central market officially debuted in  1994 and there's only 10 locations but  each of these locations are massive at  coming in at ninety thousand to one  hundred thousand square feet  central market is basically heb's  version of whole foods moving forward  hdb expanded into mexico in 1997 with  the store in monterey as you would guess  their mexican stores support the mexican  economy by sourcing 85 of their  offerings from mexican suppliers  the latest major development coming from  agb is their hdb plus stores which begin  operations in 2005. on top of offering  everything found in regular hebs plus  doors offer a larger baby department  more outdoor supplies more garden  equipment and a larger assortment of  electronic and household items today heb  generates a total of 31.2 billion annual  revenue and they employ over 135 000  employees the bud family is estimated to  be worth 10.7 billion dollars as of  january 2016. but i don't think that  money was ever the family's primary  focus and i think that's played the  secret behind her success florence  didn't start hgb to get super rich she  started it out of desperation to  financially support her family as soon  as the family became financially stable  florence turned her focus onto giving  back and that's how the family has run  the company ever since whether that be  giving back to employees customers or  the community all of their financial  success has simply been a byproduct of  them serving the community to the best  of their ability and i suspect that  charles is a lot more proud of being the  number 7 most approved ceo in the us and  the highest ranked retail ceo on the  list than he is of his 10 billion  fortune have you guys ever worked a  retail job and if so how was your  experience comment that down below  also drop a like if you think walmart  should take notes from heb  and of course consider joining our  discord community to suggest future  video ideas and consider subscribing to  see more questions logically answered  but until then i'm hari and i'll see you  guys on the next one
 

